00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:09.890
Article Two, Section Two
of the Constitution states

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.300
that the president shall
be commander-in-chief

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.200
and he shall have power to
grant reprieves and pardons

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.410
for offenses against the United States,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.710
except in cases of impeachment.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:23.480
So under that language, can
the president pardon himself?

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.287
That's what we're going to find out today.

00:00:25.287 --> 00:00:26.562
(magical music)

00:00:26.562 --> 00:00:29.062
(gavel bangs)

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.210
Hey legal eagles, welcome
back to Lawsplainer,

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.760
where we explain the most
important legal concepts

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:35.810
of the day.

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:37.420
Don't forget to subscribe
and hit that bell

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:39.800
for more legal explanations,
and don't forget

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.170
to leave comment in the
form of an objection.

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.100
Today we're going to figure
out whether the president

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.940
has the power to pardon himself.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.830
But first, let's talk
about what a pardon is.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.440
A pardon is a action
bestowed on the president

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.430
by the Constitution of the United States

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.190
that completely sets aside
or commutes the punishment

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.710
for a crime.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.570
A pardon is an admission of guilt.

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:03.960
To accept the pardon, the
recipient must essentially

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.820
admit that he committed
the underlying crime.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.130
Now, a pardon only applies to, quote,

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.460
offenses against the United States,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.620
in other words, federal crimes.

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:16.770
The president has no authority
to pardon state crimes.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.950
One of the reasons that this
is particularly important now

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.850
is that President Trump
in a news conference

00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:23.947
recently stated, "I have an absolute right

00:01:23.947 --> 00:01:25.550
"to pardon myself."

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.940
- No, I'm not above the law.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.052
I've never wanted anybody
to be above the law.

00:01:29.052 --> 00:01:32.960
But the pardons are a very
positive thing for a president.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.260
I think you see the way I'm using them.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:38.530
And yes, I do have an absolute
right to pardon myself.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:39.740
But I'll never have to do it,

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.610
because I didn't do anything wrong.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:44.240
- He also tweeted, as has
been stated by numerous

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.620
legal scholars, I have the absolute right

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:48.960
to pardon myself, but why would I do that

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.170
when I have done nothing wrong?

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.020
In the meantime, the
never ending Witch Hunt,

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:54.500
led by 13 very Angry
and Conflicted Democrats

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.590
and others continues into the mid-terms.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.880
So the question is, does
the president actually have

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:01.840
the power to pardon himself?

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.930
Well, as with most things
in constitutional law,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.850
we should start with the
text of the Constitution.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.790
Here, the text of the Constitution
provides a clear limit

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.550
on the president's pardon power.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.450
The Pardon Clause provides
that the president

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.890
cannot pardon anyone in
cases of impeachment.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.940
Now, there is an open
question as to whether

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.030
a sitting president can be
indicted or whether the only

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:24.000
remedy when a president
has committed some sort

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.120
of wrongdoing is impeachment.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.670
In fact, I did a whole video on that.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.790
There will be a link to that
down in the description.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.320
If impeachment, however,
is the only remedy

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.080
when the president has committed a crime,

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.430
then it would make sense that
the impeachment exception

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.880
to the pardon power would
prevent the president

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.700
from pardoning himself.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:42.990
And, if the president
cannot pardon himself,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.560
because that necessarily
implicates an impeachment inquiry,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.340
this principle could extend
to individuals whose actions

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.890
may be relevant to that
same impeachment inquiry.

00:02:51.890 --> 00:02:54.160
For example, Paul Manafort or Mike Flynn

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.330
or other members of
President Trump's campaign

00:02:56.330 --> 00:02:58.900
and staff have credible
allegations against them

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.080
that may implicate the
president, and therefore,

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.980
by necessity, that may
implicate impeachment,

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.180
which would prevent the
president from issuing

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.990
any kind of pardon as it regards himself

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:12.370
or as it regards the other
members of his cabinet.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.050
But the impeachment
exception to the president's

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.930
pardon power has never been
tested, so it's an open question

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.120
whether that actually provides
a limit against the president

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.020
pardoning himself or not.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.160
But on top of that, there
is also a general principle

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.930
in US law that a person
cannot be the judge

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.560
in their own case.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.840
In fact, in 1974, when
President Nixon asked

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.900
the Office of Legal Counsel,
the OLC, to determine

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.020
if he could pardon himself, the
OLC relied on that principle

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.930
in saying that President Nixon
could not pardon himself.

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:44.130
In the famous August 5th, 1974, memo,

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.640
Mary Lawton, the Acting
Assistant Attorney General,

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.120
wrote, quote, under the fundamental rule

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.900
that no one may be the
judge in his own case,

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:54.630
the president cannot pardon himself.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:58.040
Four days later, Nixon
resigned in disgrace.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.950
So given that President Nixon's staff

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.940
opined that he could not pardon himself

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.840
and that even President
Nixon would not go so far,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:08.330
as in issue a self-pardon,
that is potentially persuasive

00:04:08.330 --> 00:04:10.660
authority that the president
doesn't have the power

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:11.860
to self-pardon.

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.030
Now, there's really only
one Supreme Court case

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.340
that's on point in this particular case,

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:20.440
and that is the Ex Parte
Garland Case from 1866.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.610
This is a Supreme Court
case that often gets used

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:25.010
as an example of how
the president's ability

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.870
to pardon is basically unfettered,

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:28.990
but I think there's actually
a different principle

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.710
that is more important here.

00:04:30.710 --> 00:04:33.820
The Ex Parte Garland Case
concerned Congress's ability

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.440
to exclude members of the Confederacy

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.330
from practicing federal
law after the Civil War,

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.090
and whether the president could pardon

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:42.570
a Confederate senator.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:44.850
The Supreme Court held five to four

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.690
that the president could in fact pardon

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.406
the Confederate senator,
but more importantly,

00:04:49.406 --> 00:04:52.330
the court held unanimously
that the Supreme Court

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.090
has the authority to interpret the scope

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.450
of the pardon power.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.970
Like the bedrock Supreme
Court case of Marbury versus

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.180
Madison, Ex Parte Garland
is probably less important

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.690
for the actual outcome of
the case than the principle

00:05:04.690 --> 00:05:07.840
that the Supreme Court
has the overview authority

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.820
to interpret the Constitution in this case

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.650
and make a decision one way or another.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.720
In other words, it's less
important as to whether

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:17.150
the president has the
pardon power in question

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.080
than the fact that the
Supreme Court has the ability

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:22.090
to review the president's
pardons in the first place.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:23.620
So if I was going to try to predict

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.770
what was going to happen
if a president ever tried

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.160
to pardon himself, the way things stands,

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.900
it appears that there is
nothing that would stop

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.630
the president from pardoning
himself in the first instance.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:35.080
But under current Supreme Court case law,

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:38.110
the Supreme Court has the
power to review that pardon.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.200
And if I had to guess what
the Supreme Court would do

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.720
with a self-pardon, I would
think that the Supreme Court

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.200
would actually nullify the
president's self-pardon

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.740
on the grounds that the
president cannot be the judge

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.050
in his own case or that the
impeachment exception applies,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.670
which prevents the president's pardon

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:54.140
from going into effect.

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:56.160
Now, regardless of whether
the president actually

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:59.100
has the power to self-pardon,
it is almost universally

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:01.200
acknowledged that if a president ever did

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:04.510
try to pardon him or
herself, that it would almost

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:07.260
certainly lead to impeachment
by the House and Senate

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.950
and removal from office.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:12.440
But that is not necessarily
required by the Constitution.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:14.620
But frankly, I think I
speak for a lot of people

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:16.970
when I say that I hope
we never have to actually

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:19.550
test that legal principle in action.

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:21.440
Just to be on the safe
side, members of the current

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.810
administration might want to
brush up on their job skills,

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.450
just in case, like for example,

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:27.708
learning a new language, like Russian.

00:06:27.708 --> 00:06:30.210
I'd recommend Alina
Sychova's Skillshare course

